**Scope** = where in the text a name can be used. **Lifetime** = when the object exists while running (its *storage duration*).

- **File scope**: declared outside all functions; usable from that line to the end of the file (a global declared below a function is *not* visible in it).
- **Block scope**: inside `{ }` or a parameter; from that line to the closing `}`. `for (int i ...)`: `i` exists only in the loop.
- **Function prototype scope**: parameter names in a prototype: `void f(int n, int a[n]);` is fine, `void f(int a[n], int n);` is an error. (**Function scope**: only `goto` labels.)
- An inner declaration **hides** (shadows) an outer one with the same name; they are different variables. Rule: use the nearest declaration above, in this block or an enclosing one, then file scope.

| storage duration | who | created / destroyed | starts as |
|---|---|---|---|
| automatic | locals, parameters | each time the block is entered / left (each call, each loop pass) | **garbage** (indeterminate) |
| static | file-scope variables, anything `static`, string literals | once before `main` / at program end | **0** (`NULL`, `0.0`, members recursively) |
| allocated | `malloc`, `calloc`, `realloc` | you: allocate / `free` | `malloc` garbage, `calloc` zero |
| thread | (not in CISC220) | | |

```c
int g;                        // file scope, static duration: starts 0
int count(void) {
    static int n = 0;         // initialised once, before main runs
    int local = 0;            // automatic: re-created on every call
    n++;
    local++;
    return n * 10 + local;
}
int main(void) {
    count();
    count();
    printf("%d %d\n", count(), g);   // 31 0
    return 0;
}
```

- A `static` local keeps its value between calls but is still only visible in its function (static changes lifetime, not scope). Its initializer must be a constant (`static int v = n;` is `error: initializer element is not constant`). `strtok` keeps state this way.
- `static` on a file-scope variable or function = **internal linkage**: private to that translation unit (like Java `private static`).
- Never return the address of an automatic variable or array: it dies at `return`, the pointer **dangles** and using it is UB (gcc: `function returns address of local variable`). Use static or allocated storage.
- Uninitialised running total (`unsigned long sum;` in the notebook's `sum_of.c`) is a bug that may "work" by luck. Prefer locals + `return` over results kept in globals/statics (the `sum_of2_fails.c` bug).

- `struct tag { members; };` defines the type `struct tag` (the keyword is part of the name). Members: any type with a fixed size; arrays of constant length OK, **no variable-length array** (a *flexible array member* `int arr[];` may be last). A node refers to itself only through a **pointer**: `struct node *next;`.
- Forward declaration `struct tag;` = incomplete type: usable only as `struct tag *` (basis of opaque types).

```c
struct student {
    char name[16];                  // array member: copied by =
    int marks[3];
    double avg;
};                                  // <- semicolon!
typedef struct student student_t;  // alias, optional

void finish(struct student *s) {    // pointer: changes the caller's struct
    s->avg = (s->marks[0] + s->marks[1] + s->marks[2]) / 3.0;
}                                   // s->avg means (*s).avg

struct student a = { "Ann", { 70, 80, 90 } };   // avg empty-initialised: 0.0
struct student b = { .avg = 1.0, .name = "Bo" };  // designators
student_t c = a;                    // copies every member, name too
strcpy(c.name, "Cy");               // a.name unchanged; c.name = "Cy" is an error
finish(&a);
printf("%s %.1f %s %d %s %.1f\n", a.name, a.avg, b.name, b.marks[2], c.name, c.avg);
```

- Initialise in member order; missing members (and `{ 0 }`) are **empty-initialised**: 0, 0.0, `NULL`, recursively. Designators `{ .y = 0.2, .x = 0.1 }` (with the dot: the notebook's `{ x = 0.1 }` is a typo). Too many initialisers: error. A local struct without an initialiser holds garbage; so does `malloc`'d memory.
- `.` on a struct, **`->` on a pointer to a struct**: `p->x` is `(*p).x`; `*p.x` is wrong because `.` binds tighter than `*`. gcc: `'ptr' is a pointer; did you mean to use '->'?`
- `b = a;` and pass/return by value copy every byte: array members are copied (independent), **pointer members are copied as addresses** (both share one object: a shallow copy). An independent ("deep") copy must allocate new memory for what the pointers point at.
- Structs cannot be compared with `==`; compare member by member. `sizeof(struct tag)` may include padding (`struct { char c; int i; }` is 8 bytes): always use `sizeof`.
- Array member assigned later: `strcpy(s.name, "x")`, never `s.name = "x"` (array not assignable).
- Array of structs: `struct book shelf[3] = { {...}, {...}, {...} };`, `shelf[i].year`, `(shelf + i)->year`.
- `typedef existing alias;`: `typedef struct point2 point;`. Tagless: `typedef struct { char first; char last; } initials;` then write `initials`, never `struct initials`. Course style: keep `struct tag` for structs with visible members; use `typedef` for opaque types (`typedef struct qlstack_tag qlstack;`) and function pointers.

- `T a[n];` = n contiguous `T`s, indices 0..n-1; capacity fixed for its lifetime; no zero-length arrays. **No bounds checking**: `a[n]` compiles and is UB (may silently overwrite another variable).
- No initialiser: garbage (unless static). With a list: unmentioned elements are **0** (`= { 0 }` zeroes all; the notebook's `init3.c` wrongly says "uninitialized", and its `int x[] = {3}` has capacity 1). `int x[] = {1, 2, 3}` sets capacity 3. Too many initialisers: error. Designator `[4] = 7`. A VLA `int v[n];` takes no initialiser.
- Not assignable (`x = y` error): copy with a loop or `memcpy(dst, src, n * sizeof dst[0])` (`<string.h>`, count in **bytes**). `const int x[3]` elements are read-only.
- `sizeof(a) / sizeof(a[0])` = length **only where `a` is declared**; on a parameter it gives the pointer size (8).

```c
int sum(const int a[], size_t n) {    // a is really const int *
    int total = 0;                    // sizeof(a) would be 8 here
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        total += a[i];
    }
    return total;
}

int a[5] = { 1, 2 };                  // {1, 2, 0, 0, 0}
size_t n = sizeof(a) / sizeof(a[0]);  // 5, only where a is declared
int *p = a + 1;                       // &a[1]
printf("%d %d %d\n", *(a + 1), *a + 10, p[1]);      // 2 11 0
printf("%td %d\n", (a + n) - p, sum(a, n));         // 4 3
for (int *q = a; q < a + n; q++) {    // a + n: too-far pointer, never *
    *q *= 2;
}
int b[5];
memcpy(b, a, n * sizeof(int));        // b = a; does not compile
printf("%d %d\n", b[0], b[1]);        // 2 4
```

- The array name is a pointer to element 0: `a == &a[0]`. `p + i` moves i **elements** (scaled by `sizeof(T)`): `*(p + i)` is `p[i]`. `*a + 10` is `a[0] + 10` (`*` binds tighter than `+`).
- `a + n` is the **too-far pointer**: legal to form and compare, never to dereference. `q - p` = elements between (`ptrdiff_t`, `%td`). `p++`, `p += n`, `<` work within one array.
- Parameters `int a[]`, `int *a`, `int a[static 8]` (at least 8) are all `int *a`: a function can change the caller's elements and cannot know the length: pass `size_t n` (or `int n, int a[n]`) or use a sentinel (`'\0'`, `-1`, `NULL`).
- A function cannot return an array. Returning a local array's address dangles; return a pointer to `malloc`'d memory, fill an array the caller passes, or return a pointer into the caller's array (`return a + idx;`, caller recovers the index with `p - a`).

```c
void print(size_t rows, size_t cols, int m[rows][cols]) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < rows; i++) {           // rows outside
        for (size_t j = 0; j < cols; j++) {       // columns inside
            printf("%d ", m[i][j]);
        }
        printf("\n");
    }
}

int m[2][3] = { {1, 2, 3}, {4, 5} };   // m[1][2] is 0
print(2, 3, m);                       // 1 2 3 / 4 5 0
int *flat = &m[0][0];                 // row-major: m[i][j] = flat[i*3 + j]
printf("%d %zu\n", flat[1 * 3 + 1], sizeof(m));  // 5 24
```

- `T m[R][C]` = R rows of C, **row-major**: `m[i][j]` is element `i * C + j` from the start. Initialise row by row; missing values 0. As a parameter every size but the first is required: `int m[][3]`, or sizes first `size_t r, size_t c, int m[r][c]`. `char names[3][8]` holds 3 strings, `names[i]` is one.
- Classic loops: linear search (`return i` when found, `-1` after); reverse with `i` and `j` swapping while `i < j` (looping all n swaps twice = no change); selection sort with a min-index helper.

- A string = `char` array ending at the **first** `'\0'` (value 0, not `'0'`). Length (`strlen`, excludes `'\0'`) ≠ capacity. A string of length n needs **n + 1** chars. `s + 4` is the string starting at index 4.
- Literal `"abc"` = static, **read-only** array of 4 chars (`sizeof "abc" == 4`; `"abc\0def"`: sizeof 8, strlen 3). Modifying it is UB.
- `char s[] = "abc";` = your own copy (modifiable, `sizeof s == 4`). `char *p = "abc";` = points at the literal (`sizeof p == 8`; `p[0] = 'A'` is UB). `char s[] = "01234";` has capacity 6: no room to `strcat`.
- Missing `'\0'` (e.g. `char s[4] = {'a','b','c','d'}` or after `memcpy`): `%s`/`strlen` run past the end: UB.

| `<string.h>` | does | UB / watch |
|---|---|---|
| `size_t strlen(const char *s)` | chars before `'\0'`, O(n) | `NULL` or unterminated |
| `char *strcpy(char *dest, const char *src)` | copies incl. `'\0'`, returns `dest` | dest too small, overlap |
| `char *strcat(char *dest, const char *src)` | appends at dest's `'\0'` | needs `strlen(dest)+strlen(src)+1`; never onto itself |
| `int strcmp(const char *a, const char *b)` | <0, 0, >0 (first differing char as `unsigned char`) | test only the sign; `==` compares addresses |
| `char *strchr(s, c)` / `strrchr(s, c)` | first / last `c`, or `NULL` | test `NULL` before use |
| `char *strstr(s, sub)` | first occurrence of `sub`, or `NULL` | index = `p - s` |
| `void *memcpy(dest, src, n)` | copies n **bytes**, ignores `'\0'` | overlap is UB |
| `void *memmove(dest, src, n)` | same, overlap allowed | slower |

```c
size_t my_strlen(const char *s) {
    const char *p = s;
    while (*p != '\0') {
        p++;
    }
    return p - s;                     // pointer difference
}

char s[] = "CISC220";                 // own copy: sizeof 8, strlen 7
const char *lit = "CISC220";          // points at read-only literal
char buf[20];
strcpy(buf, s);                       // buf needs strlen(s) + 1
strcat(buf, "-A");                    // "CISC220-A"
char *c = strrchr(buf, 'C');          // last 'C', or NULL
if (c != NULL) {
    printf("%td\n", c - buf);         // 3
}
memmove(buf + 4, buf + 5, strlen(buf) - 4);  // delete index 4 (moves '\0')
printf("%s %zu %zu %d\n", buf, sizeof(s), my_strlen(lit), strcmp("abc", "abd") < 0);
```

- Order follows character codes: `"Zebra" < "apple"`, a prefix comes first (`"ab" < "abc"`), `"10" < "9"`. `<ctype.h>`: `toupper`, `tolower`, `isdigit`, `isspace`, `isalpha`.
- `int sprintf(char *buf, const char *fmt, ...)` = `printf` into `buf`; always writes `'\0'`; returns chars written (without `'\0'`); buffer too small = UB, so count first. Joining: `sprintf(d, "%s%s", a, b)` needs `strlen(a) + strlen(b) + 1`.
- `int sscanf(const char *str, const char *fmt, ...)` reads from a string: returns values assigned, stops at the first mismatch, **0** if the first conversion fails, **`EOF`** if the string ends before any conversion. Always check `== expected`.

```c
char date[11];                                   // 10 chars + '\0'
int w = sprintf(date, "%04u-%02u-%02u", 2023u, 10u, 3u);
printf("%s %d\n", date, w);                      // 2023-10-03 10

unsigned int y, m, d;
int n = sscanf("2023-01-29", "%u-%u-%u", &y, &m, &d);
printf("%d %u %u %u\n", n, y, m, d);             // 3 2023 1 29
n = sscanf("2023 -01-29", "%u-%u-%u", &y, &m, &d);
printf("%d\n", n);                               // 1 (' ' does not match '-')

char last[10], first[10];
int at;
n = sscanf("Simpson,Bart,1234", "%9[^,],%9[^,]%n", last, first, &at);
printf("%d %s %s %d\n", n, first, last, at);     // 2 Bart Simpson 12
printf("%d %d\n", sscanf("abc", "%d", &at), sscanf("  ", "%d", &at)); // 0 -1
```

- Most conversions skip leading whitespace; **`%c` and `%[` do not**. A space in the format matches any amount (even none) of whitespace; other characters must match exactly.
- `%s` one word, `%[abc]` / `%[^,]` a non-empty run of chars in / not in the set (ranges `a-z` not portable); both add `'\0'`. **Width = capacity - 1** (`char w[10]` → `%9s`). `%5c` reads exactly 5 chars, adds no `'\0'`.
- `%*d` reads but does not assign (not counted). `%n` stores chars consumed so far (`int *`), not counted. `%d` int, `%u`, `%ld`, `%lf` double, `%zu` size_t. A value that does not fit the type: UB.
- `atoi`, `atof` (`<stdlib.h>`): no error reporting (`atof("abc") == 0.0`).

`<stdlib.h>`. Allocated storage (the **heap**) lives until you `free` it; locals are on the **stack**.

| function | gives | notes |
|---|---|---|
| `void *malloc(size_t size)` | `size` **bytes**, **uninitialised** | `T *p = malloc(sizeof(T));`, array `malloc(n * sizeof(T))`; no cast needed |
| `void *calloc(size_t num, size_t size)` | `num * size` bytes, **all bits zero** | two arguments |
| `void *realloc(void *ptr, size_t new_sz)` | block resized to `new_sz` bytes **in total**, old contents kept, extra uninitialised | on success the old pointer is invalid; on `NULL` the old block is untouched; `realloc(NULL, n)` = `malloc(n)` |
| `void free(void *ptr)` | ends the lifetime | only pointers from these functions, once; `free(NULL)` does nothing |

All return `NULL` on failure (size 0: result must not be dereferenced, may be freed). Check: `if (!p) { fprintf(stderr, "...\n"); exit(EXIT_FAILURE); }`; free earlier blocks before stopping.

```c
size_t n = 0, cap = 2;
int *a = malloc(cap * sizeof(int));        // bytes! not malloc(cap)
if (!a) {
    fprintf(stderr, "out of memory\n");
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
}
for (int v = 1; v <= 5; v++) {
    if (n == cap) {                        // full: double the capacity
        int *tmp = realloc(a, 2 * cap * sizeof(int));
        if (!tmp) {                        // a is still valid here
            free(a);
            exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
        }
        a = tmp;                           // old a is now invalid
        cap *= 2;
    }
    a[n++] = v;
}
printf("%zu %zu %d\n", n, cap, a[4]);      // 5 8 5
free(a);
a = NULL;                                  // a second free(a) is now harmless
```

```c
void make(size_t n, int **out) {    // change the caller's int *
    int *buf = malloc(n * sizeof(int));
    if (!buf) {
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        buf[i] = (int) i * i;
    }
    *out = buf;                     // NOT out = buf (that changes the copy)
}

int *a = NULL;
make(4, &a);                        // a now points at heap memory
printf("%d\n", a[3]);               // 9
free(a);
```

- A function may **return** `malloc`'d memory (it outlives the call; the caller frees it), or set the caller's pointer through `T **` (`*out = buf;`, element `(*out)[i]`, never `*out[i]`).
- Array of strings: `char **w = malloc(n * sizeof(char *));` then `w[i] = malloc(strlen(s) + 1);` = 1 + n mallocs and 1 + n frees, **inner blocks first**. Struct: `malloc(sizeof(struct T))`; array of structs `a[i].m` or `(a + i)->m`.
- Notebook slip: `return_arr.c` does `malloc(len)` for an `int` array; correct is `malloc(len * sizeof(int))`.

| bug | what | fix |
|---|---|---|
| leak | block never freed (only pointer lost or overwritten) | one `free` per allocation |
| use after free | `*p` after `free(p)` (also via another pointer to it) | free after the last use; unlink then free |
| double free | `free` twice on the same block (UB) | `free(p); p = NULL;` (protects only `p`) |
| invalid free | `free` of a local, a literal, `argv[i]`, or a pointer into the middle of a block | free exactly what malloc returned |
| too small | `malloc(n)` for n ints; `malloc(strlen(s))` (no `'\0'`) | `n * sizeof(T)`, `strlen + 1` |
| uninitialised read | reading `malloc`'d memory before writing it | write first or `calloc` |
| realloc leak | `p = realloc(p, n);` loses `p` if it fails | `tmp`, test, then `p = tmp` |
| dangling | returning a local's address; pointer into a block that was realloc'd/freed | heap memory, update pointers |

valgrind: compile with `-g`, run `valgrind --leak-check=full ./prog args`. "definitely lost" = leak; "Invalid read/write" = bad access; clean = `All heap blocks were freed` and `0 errors`. "It printed the right value" never proves UB-free code.

Why: C strings have O(n) `strlen`, no capacity info, manual memory. `qstr` knows its length (O(1)), its capacity, and grows itself. Every `qstr` is **two heap blocks** (struct + `data`); `data` must never point at a local array (`badstring`). A `qstr` may contain `'\0'` inside (then `strlen(s->data) < s->length`), so copying uses `memcpy`/`memmove` with `length + 1`, never `strcpy`.

```c
struct qstr {
    size_t length;      // chars, not counting the final '\0'
    size_t capacity;    // size of data array, >= length + 1
    char *data;         // heap array, data[length] == '\0'
};
struct qstr *qstr_alloc(size_t capacity) {
    struct qstr *q = malloc(sizeof(struct qstr));
    if (!q) {
        return NULL;
    }
    if (capacity == 0) {
        capacity = 1;
    }
    q->data = calloc(capacity, 1);      // all '\0': empty string
    if (!q->data) {
        free(q);                        // don't leak the struct
        return NULL;
    }
    q->length = 0;
    q->capacity = capacity;
    return q;
}

struct qstr *qstr_concat(struct qstr *lhs, const struct qstr *rhs) {
    if (!lhs) {
        return NULL;
    }
    if (!rhs) {
        return lhs;
    }
    if (!qstr_ensure_capacity(lhs, lhs->length + rhs->length + 1)) {
        return NULL;                    // realloc failed
    }
    // memmove: lhs == rhs makes the ranges overlap
    memmove(lhs->data + lhs->length, rhs->data, rhs->length + 1);
    lhs->length += rhs->length;
    return lhs;
}
struct qstr *qstr_remrange(struct qstr *s, size_t start, size_t stop) {
    if (!s || start > stop || stop > s->length) {
        return NULL;
    }
    memmove(s->data + start, s->data + stop, s->length - stop + 1);
    s->length -= stop - start;
    return s;
}
void qstr_destroy(struct qstr *s) {
    if (!s) {
        return;
    }
    free(s->data);                      // the array first,
    free(s);                            // then the struct
}
```

| function | key points |
|---|---|
| `qstr_new()` | `qstr_alloc(QSTR_DEFAULT_CAPACITY)` (8) |
| `qstr_fromcstr(s)` | capacity `strlen(s) + 1`, copy chars, set `length` |
| `qstr_copy(s)` | **deep copy**: new struct **and** new array (same capacity), copy `length + 1` bytes; `*t = *s` would be shallow (shared `data`, double free later) |
| `qstr_clear(s)` | `data[0] = '\0'`, `length = 0`; capacity unchanged |
| `qstr_get(s, i)` / `qstr_set(s, i, c)` | `NULL` if `!s` or `i >= s->length`; else `s->data + i` (test before `*`) |
| `qstr_ensure_capacity(s, cap)` | if too small: `realloc` into `buf`, test, then `s->data = buf; s->capacity = cap` |
| `qstr_assign(lhs, rhs)` | `lhs == rhs`: nothing; else ensure `rhs->length + 1`, `memcpy` `length + 1` bytes, copy `length` |
| `qstr_concat(lhs, rhs)` | ensure `lhs->length + rhs->length + 1`; `memmove` (self-concat overlaps) |
| `qstr_remrange(s, start, stop)` | removes `[start, stop)`; needs `start <= stop <= length`; `memmove` `length - stop + 1` bytes (incl. `'\0'`) |
| `qstr_destroy(s)` | `NULL`: return; `free(s->data)` **then** `free(s)` |

- Header `qstr.h` (guard, `<stddef.h>`, struct, prototypes) + `qstr.c` (definitions); compile `gcc -Wall -o prog prog.c qstr.c`. Prefix `qstr_` avoids name clashes; functions take/return `struct qstr *`, `NULL` = error.
- Invariant: `capacity >= length + 1` and `data[length] == '\0'`, so `s->data` works with `<string.h>` and `puts`.
- `const struct qstr *s` protects the members, not the chars `data` points at (so `qstr_set` compiles).
- **Opaque type**: header has only `struct qstr;`, the definition lives in `qstr.c`; users cannot touch members (need accessor functions for length/data). Passing by pointer keeps users **binary compatible**: the struct can change size without recompiling user code (relink only). `FILE` is an opaque type.

Stack = LIFO: `push` and `pop` at the top, both **O(1)** with linked nodes. Each node's `next` points at the node below; the bottom node's `next` is `NULL`.

```c
typedef struct qlstack_tag qlstack;   // in qlstack.h (opaque type)

struct node {                         // in qlstack.c
    int elem;
    struct node *next;                // pointer to the node below
};
struct qlstack_tag {
    struct node *top;                 // NULL when empty
    size_t size;
};
qlstack *qlstack_push(qlstack *s, int elem) {
    if (!s) {
        return NULL;
    }
    struct node *n = malloc(sizeof(struct node));  // never a local node
    if (!n) {
        return NULL;
    }
    n->elem = elem;
    n->next = s->top;                 // 1. connect the new node first
    s->top = n;                       // 2. then move top
    s->size++;
    return s;
}
qlstack *qlstack_pop(qlstack *s, int *popped) {
    if (!s || !s->top) {              // the notebook forgets the empty check
        return NULL;
    }
    struct node *old = s->top;
    if (popped) {
        *popped = old->elem;          // save data before free
    }
    s->top = old->next;               // unlink,
    s->size--;
    free(old);                        // then free
    return s;
}
void qlstack_free(qlstack *s) {
    if (!s) {
        return;
    }
    struct node *n = s->top;
    while (n) {
        struct node *next = n->next;  // save the link before free(n)
        free(n);
        n = next;
    }
    free(s);
}
```

- `qlstack_new`: `malloc(sizeof(qlstack))`, `NULL` on failure, **set `top = NULL` and `size = 0`** (malloc does not). `is_empty`: `s->size == 0` (`bool`); `size`: `s->size`. Peek: `s->top->elem` only if not empty.
- Push order: **connect the new node first, then redirect the old pointer**; the other order makes the node point at itself and loses the stack. A local `struct node n;` dangles after return.
- Walk: `for (struct node *p = s->top; p != NULL; p = p->next)`. `a->next->elem` is one node further. Every chain must end in `NULL`.
- Notebook slips: the header says `qlstack_init` but the code is `qlstack_new`; `qlgstack.c` shows `int elem` (must be `void *elem`); pop never checks for an empty stack.

### Generic stack (`qlgstack`)
`struct node { void *elem; struct node *next; };` stores **addresses**, never copies: each element must outlive its time in the stack (usually its own `malloc`; pushing `&x` repeatedly gives many nodes pointing at one object).

```c
typedef void (*print_func)(const void *elem);   // pointer-to-function type

void print_int(const void *elem) {
    const int *p = elem;              // void * converts without a cast
    printf("%d", *p);
}
void free_int(void *elem) {           // not const: it calls free
    free(elem);
}

int *x = malloc(sizeof(int));         // each element its own heap object
*x = 42;
push(s, x);                           // stack stores the address only
void *p;                              // pop needs a void **: &p
pop(s, &p);                           // not an int ** (&x)
print_func f = print_int;             // or &print_int
f(p);                                 // or (*f)(p); prints 42
void (*g)(void *) = free_int;         // without the typedef
g(p);                                 // popped element is yours to free
```

- Pointer to function: write the declaration and replace the name with `(*p)`: `void (*p)(const void *elem);`. Assign `p = f;` or `p = &f;`, call `p(x)` or `(*p)(x)`. `typedef void (*qlgstack_print_func)(const void *elem);`
- `qlgstack_print(pre, s, post, print_fn)`: `NULL` prints each element as `?`. `qlgstack_free(s, free_fn)`: frees each element with `free_fn` (if not `NULL`), each node, then the stack. Pass `NULL` if the elements are not heap blocks (e.g. `argv` strings).
- Who frees: nodes and stack → stack code; elements still in the stack → your free function; popped elements → you. `void *` converts to and from any object pointer, but an `int **` is not a `void **`: pop into a `void *p` with `&p` (the notebook `demo.c` passes `&val1`, an `int **`, and gcc warns).

### Deleting a node from a singly linked list
```c
struct node {
    int elem;
    struct node *next;
};
// removes the first node holding target; returns the (new) head
struct node *remove_first(struct node *head, int target) {
    struct node *prev = NULL;
    struct node *cur = head;
    while (cur != NULL && cur->elem != target) {   // find
        prev = cur;
        cur = cur->next;
    }
    if (cur == NULL) {                // empty list or not found
        return head;
    }
    if (prev == NULL) {               // removing the head
        head = cur->next;
    }
    else {
        prev->next = cur->next;       // middle or last: unlink
    }
    free(cur);                        // free AFTER unlinking
    return head;
}
```

- Find (`prev` one step behind `cur`), **unlink**, save any data, **then** `free(cur)`; never read `cur->...` after `free`. `prev->next = NULL` would cut off (leak) the rest.
- A function that may remove the head must return the new head (or take `struct node **`). Test: empty list, one node, head, last node, not found.
